BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — We're not the only ones mapping bees (com
 munity)
DTSTART:20260927T150000Z
DTEND:20260927T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T200000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-mapmydca-20260927@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot — new 
 content this run (Sep 25 2026\, off-cycle run after a scheduling gap\; the
  previous scheduled run was Sep 6 2026\, which only filled through Sep 20)
 . Pulled from the standing FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §7 idea of name-check
 ing kindred citizen-science projects — different angle from the Sep 13 A
 uburn/AIA post\, this one spotlights Julia Mahood / mapmydca.com (already 
 a CONTACTED lead in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md\, emailed 2026-07-25\, awaiting
  reply).\n\nPost text:\nSaveTheHives isn't the only citizen-science projec
 t asking people to help map honey bees. Julia Mahood — a master beekeepe
 r and Georgia's 2018 Beekeeper of the Year — runs mapmydca.com\, which t
 racks drone congregation areas (DCAs): the specific spots where drones gat
 her to mate with queens\, found using mechanical drones carrying queen-phe
 romone lures. Different question than ours (where drones meet vs. where co
 lonies live)\, same spirit: real data built by people willing to go look.
 \n\nDCA mapping is actually on our own roadmap too — marked "Coming soon
 " in the app. Kindred projects like this are exactly why we're excited to 
 build it. SaveTheHives.org\n\nImage: no asset directly depicts DCA mapping
 /drone lures — fallback to images/facebook/map-screenshot-full_fb-square
 -1080x1080.jpg (also _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)\, last used Sep 13\, ~2-w
 eek gap\, reasonable reuse as a generic "our own map" visual. Flagged for 
 Ronnie to swap in something DCA-specific if a better asset turns up.\n\nAl
 t text: "Screenshot of the full SaveTheHives map showing hive pin location
 s across the region."\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as plain text — a p
 hoto is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Su
 n Sep 27\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content this run. No individual resear
 cher to expert-check (Mahood isn't an academic co-author on a paper\, she'
 s the project founder\, already tracked as her own KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md 
 entry) — the Sunday slot doesn't require the expert-authorship check any
 way (Monday-only per the task instructions).
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Wild honey bees just got reclassified as 
 Endangered in Europe (research hook)
DTSTART:20260928T150000Z
DTEND:20260928T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T200000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-honeybeewatch-iucn-20260928@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot — fresh res
 earch-hook search this run (Sep 25 2026\, off-cycle run\; resumes the sear
 ch the Sep 6 2026 run deferred to "starting Sep 21\," which never happened
  due to a scheduling gap).\n\nExpert-authorship / organization check: STRO
 NG MATCH — not an individual author but the leading organization itself.
  This reassessment was led by Honey Bee Watch\, an international bee-resea
 rch coalition already listed in KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md under "European non
 -managed/native honey bee conservation — LEAD" ("international research 
 coalition studying free-living honey bees\, with a published citizen-scien
 ce monitoring protocol") — flagged there as one of three reasonable firs
 t emails for a European partnership/cross-promotion conversation. Worth up
 grading from a cold name on a list to an actual outreach target given this
  fresh visibility. Lead coordinator: Dr. Arrigo Moro (University of Galway
  & Honey Bee Watch\, IE)\; 14 assessors total across European institutions
  — none of the other 13 match existing KEY_PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md entries.\n
 \nDating note for Ronnie: the underlying IUCN Red List reassessment/press 
 release is dated 11 Oct 2025 (Galway\, Ireland) — not brand new — but 
 it's actively recirculating in current Sep 2026 bee-news coverage (Bee Spa
 ced Digest Sep 22 2026\, phys.org\, The Conversation\, APIMONDIA)\, which 
 is how it surfaced in this run's search. Framed in the post copy as "just 
 got reclassified" for social read-through\; flag if that framing feels too
  close to implying it's this week's news specifically.\n\nPost text:\nNew 
 scientific news validates the whole reason a map like this needs to exist.
  Wild\, free-living honey bee colonies in the European Union have been rec
 lassified as Endangered on the IUCN Red List — the first time that's hap
 pened. For over a decade\, most scientists assumed wild honey bees had sim
 ply gone extinct there. A small international research coalition called Ho
 ney Bee Watch spent years tracking down colonies living in tree cavities\,
  chimneys\, even abandoned mailboxes to prove otherwise. Now the data says
  those populations could fall by more than half within 10 years without he
 lp.\n\nIt's a sobering find\, and also exactly why projects like SaveTheHi
 ves matter: you can't protect a population nobody's tracking. Every hive l
 ogged here does the same kind of work Honey Bee Watch's researchers did ac
 ross Europe — just closer to home. SaveTheHives.org\n\nImage: no dedicat
 ed asset for European wild bees — fallback to images/facebook/honeybee-o
 n-comb_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (also _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)\, last us
 ed Sep 14\, ~2-week gap\, reasonable reuse.\n\nAlt text: "Close-up of a ho
 ney bee on honeycomb."\n\nLink: keep the trailing "SaveTheHives.org" as pl
 ain text — a photo is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSour
 ce: Honey Bee Watch press release\, "Wild Honey Bees Receive New IUCN Red 
 List Status" (Galway\, Ireland\, 11 Oct 2025)\, coloss.org. IUCN Red List 
 entry: www.iucnredlist.org/species/42463639/277757621. Underlying populati
 on-decline data: Kohl & Rutschmann\, https://doi.org/10.32942/X2SW5M.\n\nS
 uggested timing: Mon Sep 28\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — A few ground rules once you've found one 
 (practical/safety)
DTSTART:20260930T150000Z
DTEND:20260930T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T200000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-groundrules-20260930@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Wednesday practical/recruiting slot — n
 ew content this run (Sep 25 2026)\, no ready post existed for this slot. F
 irst safety/etiquette-focused post in the rotation — §5 lists "safety" 
 as a standing Wednesday content type that hadn't been used yet.\n\nPost te
 xt:\nFinding a wild colony is the fun part. Here's how to not mess it up o
 nce you have: keep your distance — a hollow tree or wall cavity is a liv
 ed-in home\, not a photo prop. Don't tap\, smoke\, or pry at the entrance 
 to get a better look. Watch from a few feet back and let the bees come and
  go undisturbed\; that's plenty to confirm activity and get a usable photo
  for the log. If it's on someone else's property\, always ask first.\n\nNo
 ne of this takes gear or experience — just patience and the same respect
  you'd give any wild animal's home. Log what you find: SaveTheHives.org 
 🐝\n\nImage: images/facebook/learn-terrain-c_fb-square-1080x1080.jpg (al
 so _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg) — unused in any post so far. Not visually
  inspected this run (image files aren't viewable from this environment) 
 — flagged for Ronnie to eyeball before posting and swap for learn-terrai
 n-a (also unused) if it's a better fit for a "keep your distance" framing.
 \n\nAlt text: "Wooded terrain from the SaveTheHives Learn tab\, used to il
 lustrate field technique." (placeholder — adjust once the actual image i
 s confirmed)\n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as plain text — a photo is a
 ttached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Wed Sep 30
 \, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNote: new content this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — Every ‘still there’ is as valuable as
  every new find (community)
DTSTART:20261004T150000Z
DTEND:20261004T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T200000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-validate-emphasis-20261004@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Sunday community/reflective slot — new 
 content this run (Sep 25 2026). Directly acts on the standing strategic no
 te in FACEBOOK_STARTER_PACK.md §4 (from the Jul 21 2026 Tarpy meeting): V
 alidate should be weighted more heavily in content than the launch sequenc
 e's mix leaned toward. First post since Post 3 (the launch sequence's Vali
 date-focused entry\, Jul 26 2026) built specifically around this message.
 \n\nPost text:\nIt's tempting to think the most exciting way to help is fi
 nding a brand-new hive. But confirming one that's already on the map — t
 apping a pin\, hitting Validate\, answering "yes\, still there" — is jus
 t as valuable to the researchers using this data. Over 1\,150 legacy recor
 ds go back to 2008\; most haven't been checked on in years. A single confi
 rmation turns an old\, stale record into current\, usable data.\n\nIf you'
 ve got five minutes and a pin near you\, that's the whole ask. SaveTheHive
 s.org\n\nImage: SaveTheHives_map_screenshot_cropped.jpg (repo root) — la
 st used for launch-sequence Post 3 (Jul 26 2026)\, over two months ago\, t
 hematically on-point for a Validate-focused post.\n\nAlt text: "Screenshot
  of the SaveTheHives map showing hive pin locations around Raleigh\, NC."
 \n\nLink: keep the trailing URL as plain text — a photo is attached so n
 o preview card will generate.\n\nSuggested timing: Sun Oct 4\, 11am-2pm ET
 \n\nNote: new content this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SaveTheHives FB Post — The losses are still high — but they di
 dn't have to happen (research hook)
DTSTART:20261005T150000Z
DTEND:20261005T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T200000Z
UID:savethehives-fbpost-auburn-survey-20261005@savethehives.org
CATEGORIES:SaveTheHives,Facebook
COLOR:purple
DESCRIPTION:ONGOING ROTATION — Monday science/mission slot — fresh res
 earch-hook search this run (Sep 25 2026)\, second Monday slot filled this 
 run since two Mondays fall in the current 2-week window after the scheduli
 ng gap.\n\nExpert-authorship check: PASSED\, direct individual match. The 
 2025–26 U.S. Beekeeping Survey (Auburn University\, Oregon State Univers
 ity\, Apiary Inspectors of America) is led by Geoff Williams\, director of
  Auburn's Bee Center — Geoff Williams (Auburn) is already listed in KEY_
 PEOPLE_CONTACTS.md's Batch 1 university outreach list (not yet contacted).
  He's not just an adjacent academic — he's literally the person producin
 g the national colony-loss numbers SaveTheHives' own messaging leans on (t
 he 30–50% loss-rate stat in the Genetic Goldmine explainer and Post 4). 
 Strong reason to prioritize his outreach over the rest of the untouched Ba
 tch 1 list.\n\nPost text:\nFresh numbers on the state of managed beekeepin
 g: the 2025–26 U.S. Beekeeping Survey (Auburn University\, Oregon State
 \, and the Apiary Inspectors of America) found beekeepers lost 39.9% of th
 eir colonies this year — an improvement from the past two years' record 
 55%+ losses\, but still with winter losses running about 10 points above w
 hat beekeepers consider sustainable for their operations.\n\nManaged colon
 ies survive because beekeepers intervene constantly. The ones that don't n
 eed that intervention — the non-managed colonies we map — are the ones
  worth understanding. Every pin here is a lead on how that's possible. Sav
 eTheHives.org\n\nImage: images/facebook/oak-tree-colony_fb-square-1080x108
 0.jpg (also _fb-portrait-1080x1350.jpg)\, last used Sep 20\, ~15-day gap\,
  reasonable reuse — fits the "colonies that don't need help" contrast.\n
 \nAlt text: "A honey bee colony's entrance in a cavity of an old oak tree.
 "\n\nLink: keep the trailing "SaveTheHives.org" as plain text — a photo 
 is attached so no preview card will generate.\n\nSource: Auburn University
  / Oregon State University / Apiary Inspectors of America\, "2025–26 U.S
 . Beekeeping Survey" results\, reported via wire.auburn.edu (Jun 2026) and
  American Bee Journal.\n\nSuggested timing: Mon Oct 5\, 11am-2pm ET\n\nNot
 e: new content this run.
LOCATION:facebook.com/savethehives
STATUS:CONFIRMED
END:VEVENT
